7:T4a9,Source: SIG-PAPER-2023-HK, ePrint 2023/914 full text (PDF downloaded
2026-10-11; the PDF states no revision date). Figure 1 (p. 3) maps the
positive and negative relations. Section 1.1 (pp. 3-5) states that SDLog is
equivalent to key-only security when the hash is a programmable random oracle
(p. 3), the free-base one-more discrete-logarithm condition (p. 4), and the
ways around the results: breaking that assumption, a new non-algebraic proof
technique, or an additional idealized model such as the AGM or GGM (p. 5).
Section 2.3 (p. 7) restricts the analysis to fully black-box reductions, and
Section 3.2 (pp. 13-14) models the conversion function as a bijective random
oracle between two standard-model maps. Theorems 1 and 2 (Section 4.1,
pp. 14-15) rule out algebraic reductions from DLog to SDLog that do not
program, or do not rewind, unless the free-base one-more discrete-logarithm
assumption fails; Theorem 3 (Section 4.2, pp. 17-18) rules out algebraic
reductions from key-only to chosen-message security that do not program,
unless DLog is easy. The result covers algebraic black-box reductions only; it
is not an attack on ECDSA and not a lower bound for arbitrary reductions.8:T464,Let \( E \) be an elliptic curve with a point \( G \) of prime order \( n \),
\( f(R) = x(R) \bmod n \), and \( H : \{0,1\}^* \to \mathbb{Z}_n \). ECDSA
signatures on \( m \) under \( D = dG \) are pairs \( (s, t) \) with
\( t = f\big(s^{-1}(H(m)G + tD)\big) \) and \( s, t \neq 0 \). Security is
EUF-CMA: an adversary given \( D \) and a signing oracle wins by outputting a
valid signature on a message never queried. The target is a reduction
\( \mathcal{R} \) such that for every adversary \( \mathcal{A} \) making
\( q_s \) signing and \( q_h \) hash queries with advantage \( \varepsilon \),
\( \mathcal{R}^{\mathcal{A}} \) computes \( d \) from \( D \) with advantage
at least \( \varepsilon^{c}/\mathrm{poly}(q_s, q_h) \) for a constant \( c \),
in time polynomial in that of \( \mathcal{A} \). \( \mathcal{R} \) may program
\( H \) as a random oracle; it must use \( E \), its encoding and \( f \) as
given, without generic-group or random-function idealization. By
SIG-PAPER-2023-HK, such an \( \mathcal{R} \) cannot be both algebraic and
black-box unless the free-base one-more discrete-logarithm assumption fails.9:T44e,Let \( H \) be a random oracle and
\( \Sigma = (\mathsf{Gen}^H, \mathsf{Sign}^H, \mathsf{Ver}^H) \) a signature
scheme with security parameter \( \lambda \) and overwhelming correctness.
Write \( \ell_{pk} \) and \( \ell_\sigma \) for the bit lengths of public
keys and signatures. Adversaries are computationally unbounded and are
charged only for oracle queries.

Lower-bound direction: for every one-time scheme with
\( \ell_{pk} + \ell_\sigma = o(\lambda^2/\log\lambda) \), and every many-time
scheme with \( \ell_\sigma = o(\lambda^2/\log\lambda) \), there is an
adversary making \( 2^{o(\lambda)} \) oracle queries that wins the one-time
(respectively chosen-message) existential-unforgeability game with
probability \( 1/\mathrm{poly}(\lambda) \). No restriction is placed on how
\( \mathsf{Ver} \) chooses its queries.

Construction direction: a scheme with
\( \ell_\sigma = o(\lambda^2/\log\lambda) \) (one-time:
\( \ell_{pk} + \ell_\sigma \)) such that every adversary making at most
\( 2^{\delta\lambda} \) oracle queries, for a constant \( \delta > 0 \), forges
with negligible probability.a:T471,ePrint 2023/914 PDF (downloaded 2026-10-11; the PDF states no revision date): abstract (p. 1) for the conclusion that a meaningful proof without strong idealization is unlikely; Section 1 (p. 2) for the observation that all existing (EC)DSA proofs program f and for the question, with footnote 2 keeping the hash a programmable random oracle; Figure 1 (p. 3) for the map of positive and negative results; Section 1.1 (pp. 4-5) for the free-base one-more discrete-logarithm condition and the ways around the results (breaking that assumption, a new non-algebraic proof technique, or a further idealized model such as the AGM or GGM); Section 2.3 (p. 7) for fully black-box reductions; Section 3.2 (pp. 13-14) for the modeling of f against algebraic algorithms; Theorems 1 and 2 (Section 4.1, pp. 14-15), Theorem 3 (Section 4.2, pp. 17-18), and Theorems 4 and 5 (Section 4.3, pp. 22-23). The authors' TCC 2023 talk slides (https://iacr.org/submit/files/slides/2023/tcc/tcc2023/93/slides.pdf), checked 2026-10-10, summarize the same results: slide 7 gives the results table and slides 9 and 10 the assumption and the ways around the result.b:T5b6,- **EUF-CMA and SUF-CMA**: strong unforgeability (SUF-CMA) also forbids a new
  signature on a message that was already signed.
- **One-time and many-time**: a one-time signature is secure for a single
  message; Lamport's scheme needs only a one-way function
  (SIG-PAPER-1979-LAMPORT).
- **Stateful versus stateless**: a Merkle tree authenticates many one-time keys
  under one root, but the signer must remember which keys it has used
  (SIG-PAPER-1989-MERKLE); a stateless scheme needs no such memory
  (SIG-PAPER-2019-SPHINCSPLUS).
- **Proof model**: standard model; random-oracle model (ROM), where a hash
  function is modelled as a random function; quantum ROM (QROM), where the
  adversary may query the hash in superposition.
- **Hash-and-sign versus Fiat–Shamir**: hash-and-sign applies a trapdoor
  function to a hash of the message (SIG-PAPER-1996-PSS, SIG-PAPER-2008-GPV).
  Fiat–Shamir turns an identification protocol into a signature by hashing the
  commitment and the message into the challenge (SIG-PAPER-1986-FS).
- **Nonces and sampling**: randomized signing must keep its per-signature
  randomness secret and well distributed; deterministic nonces remove a common
  failure (SIG-PAPER-2011-ED25519). Lattice signers must also avoid leaking the
  key through the signature distribution, by rejection sampling or trapdoor
  Gaussian sampling.
- **Tightness**: how much security a reduction loses relative to the
  underlying assumption.c:T5a6,- **RSA and factoring**: the randomized PSS encoding in the ROM
  (SIG-PAPER-1996-PSS) and standard-model signatures under strong RSA
  (SIG-PAPER-1999-CS).
- **Discrete logarithms**: Schnorr signatures via Fiat–Shamir
  (SIG-PAPER-1991-SCHNORR) and Ed25519 (SIG-PAPER-2011-ED25519). ECDSA is not
  a Fiat–Shamir scheme; its proofs idealize part of the conversion function
  (SIG-PAPER-2016-FKP) or the group (SIG-PAPER-2022-GS). FIPS 186-5 specifies
  RSA, ECDSA, and EdDSA and keeps DSA for verification only
  (SIG-PAPER-2023-FIPS186-5).
- **Pairings**: signatures of one group element in the ROM
  (SIG-PAPER-2001-BLS) and short signatures without random oracles under q-SDH
  (SIG-PAPER-2004-BB).
- **Hash-based**: from Lamport and Merkle to the stateless SPHINCS+ hypertree
  (SIG-PAPER-2019-SPHINCSPLUS), standardized as SLH-DSA
  (SIG-PAPER-2024-FIPS205). They rest on hash functions alone, but SLH-DSA
  signatures are 7,856 bytes or more.
- **Lattice hash-and-sign**: GPV trapdoor preimage sampling
  (SIG-PAPER-2008-GPV), instantiated over NTRU lattices in Falcon
  (SIG-PAPER-2020-FALCON).
- **Lattice Fiat–Shamir with aborts**: rejection sampling hides the secret
  without a trapdoor (SIG-PAPER-2009-LYU); Gaussian rejection sampling then
  gives SIS- and LWE-based signatures of norm \( \tilde O(n) \) (SIG-PAPER-2012-LYU),
  leading to Dilithium (SIG-PAPER-2018-DILITHIUM), standardized as ML-DSA
  (SIG-PAPER-2024-FIPS204).d:T543,Parameters below occur in efficiency or security bounds. Each meaning is
scoped to its stated regime. Measured quantities are named in words; protocol
objects are defined where used. Source aliases do not equate different regimes.

| Symbol | Meaning | Regime | Paper aliases |
|---|---|---|---|
| \(\lambda\) | Computational security parameter. | Signature security and random-oracle lower bounds. | No paper spelling asserted. |
| \(q\) | Coefficient modulus. | Lattice signatures. | SIG-PAPER-2018-DILITHIUM writes \(q\) |
| \(n\) | Lattice dimension or polynomial degree, as specified by the construction. | Lattice bounds: dimension or polynomial degree. ECDSA body text separately defines the group order; these parameters are not equated. | SIG-PAPER-2012-LYU writes \(n\); SIG-PAPER-2018-DILITHIUM writes \(n\) |
| \(k\) | Module row rank. | Dilithium parameters. | SIG-PAPER-2018-DILITHIUM writes \(k\) |
| \(\ell\) | Module column rank. | Dilithium parameters. | SIG-PAPER-2018-DILITHIUM writes \(\ell\) |
| \(q_s\) | Adversary signing-oracle query count. | Unforgeability reductions. | No paper spelling asserted. |
| \(q_h\) | Adversary hash-oracle query count. | Unforgeability reductions. | No paper spelling asserted. |
| \(\varepsilon\) | Forgery advantage in the stated game. | Security reductions. | No paper spelling asserted. |1:["$","$L6",null,{"catalog":{"catalogVersion":"signature-dossier-v3-atomic-contributions","constructions":[{"adaptive_security":null,"api_style":null,"associated_data":null,"assumption_family":"factoring","assumption_id":"ASM-RSA","assumption_ids":["ASM-RSA"],"assumption_name":"RSA inversion","authors":["Ronald L. Rivest","Adi Shamir","Leonard M. Adleman"],"base_signature":null,"block_size":null,"bootstrapping":null,"capabilities":["public-verification","message-recovery-encoding"],"ciphertext_security":null,"circuit_class":null,"client_storage":null,"communication":null,"construction_family":"rsa","correctness":null,"corruption_model":null,"decapsulation_cost":null,"decrypt_cost":{},"decrypt_pairings":"","decryption_failure":null,"encapsulation_cost":null,"exactness":null,"id":"SIG-CONSTRUCTION-1978-RSA","identifiable_abort":null,"key_size":null,"large_universe":null,"misuse_resistance":null,"multi_use_attributes":null,"noise_management":null,"nonce_generation":"none","nonce_requirement":null,"nonce_size":null,"normative_status":"historical foundation; not a secure encoding by itself","object_type":null,"online":null,"output_compatibility":null,"packing":null,"paper_title":"A Method for Obtaining Digital Signatures and Public-Key Cryptosystems","paper_url":"https://people.csail.mit.edu/rivest/Rsapaper.pdf","parallelizable":null,"plaintext_space":null,"policy_class":null,"post_quantum_mechanism":null,"preprocessing":null,"primitive":"digital_signature","privacy_model":null,"proof_model":"pre-modern heuristic security","quantum_security":null,"query_communication":null,"resilience":null,"response_communication":null,"robustness":null,"security_mode":"public-key","security_model":"deterministic algebraic relation","security_notion":"not EUF-CMA as written","server_model":null,"server_work":null,"setup_model":null,"signer_model":"single_signer","signing_cost