y analysis","Hybrid profiles that explicitly pay multiple verification or bandwidth costs"],"dossier_type":"barrier","escape_hatches":["new compact hash authentication structures","simpler lattice sampling","new conservative algebraic assumptions","profile-specific co-design"],"evidence":"primary_sources_compared","excludes":["Treating selection or standardization status as proof that all three families have the same implementation and assumption profile","Claiming a cross-family size winner without fixing parameter set, encoding, and security target"],"id":"SIG-BARRIER-001","scope":{"axes":"signature size assumption profile and signing implementation","families":"standardized post-quantum signatures at the cutoff"},"status":"scoped_literature_barrier","targets":[],"title":"Compactness, conservative assumptions, and simple constant-time signing are separate design axes"},"primaryUrl":null,"sections":[{"content":"This is a taxonomy barrier, not a lower bound. It prevents an invalid apples-to-oranges inference while leaving the normalized target open.","heading":"Evidence note"}],"slug":"compactness-conservative-ass","status":"scoped_literature_barrier","subtitle":"","summary":"This is a taxonomy barrier, not a lower bound. It prevents an invalid apples-to-oranges inference while leaving the normalized target open.","title":"Compactness, conservative assumptions, and simple constant-time signing are separate design axes","type":"barrier","venue":null,"year":null,"sourcePath":"data/signature-catalog.json#SIG-BARRIER-001"},{"evidence":"primary_source_checked","id":"SIG-BARRIER-002","keywords":[],"metadata":{"claim":"Hartmann and Kiltz show by meta-reductions that an algebraic security reduction for ECDSA from the discrete-logarithm problem can exist only if it may program the conversion function, assuming a free-base one-more discrete-logarithm assumption.","does_not_exclude":["Non-algebraic or non-black-box reductions","Proofs from stronger assumptions over the curve group","Rewinding proofs in idealized models that make the conversion function programmable, such as the bijective random oracle proof of Fersch, Kiltz and Poettering","Proofs in the generic group model or the algebraic group model"],"dossier_type":"barrier","escape_hatches":["non-algebraic reductions","non-black-box reductions","stronger assumptions","generic or algebraic group model"],"evidence":"primary_source_checked","excludes":["Algebraic black-box reductions from discrete logarithms to key-only, and hence chosen-message, unforgeability of GenDSA, ECDSA included, that do not program the conversion function, modeled as a bijective random oracle between two standard-model maps","Algebraic black-box reductions of this kind that do not rewind the adversary, even when they may program the conversion function"],"id":"SIG-BARRIER-002","scope":{"notions":"key-only and chosen-message unforgeability","reductions":"algebraic black-box reductions from discrete logarithms","scheme":"unmodified ECDSA and GenDSA"},"status":"scoped_literature_barrier","targets":["SIG-OP-002","SIG-OP-005"],"title":"Algebraic reductions for ECDSA must program the conversion function"},"primaryUrl":null,"sections":[{"content":"$7","heading":"Evidence note"}],"slug":"algebraic-reductions-for-ecd","status":"scoped_literature_barrier","subtitle":"","summary":"Source: SIG-PAPER-2023-HK, ePrint 2023/914 full text (PDF downloaded 2026-10-11; the PDF states no revision date). Figure 1 (p. 3) maps the positive and negative relations. Section 1.1 (pp. 3-5) states that SDLog is equivalent to key-only security when the hash is a programmable random oracle (p. 3), the free-base one-more discrete-logarithm condition (p. 4), and the ways around the results: breaking that assumption, a new…","title":"Algebraic reductions for ECDSA must program the conversion function","type":"barrier","venue":null,"year":null,"sourcePath":"data/signature-catalog.json#SIG-BARRIER-002"},{"contributions":["SIG-RESULT-1978-RSA-PUBL